/usr/bin/expand from the Micronix 1.6 distribution

There is no source for expand, so this is the disassembly, and expand.c is
that disassembly read back into C.

    disas -f expand.ctl expand.dist

expand.dist is the binary off inode 55 of disks/dist/1012-8_dist_2.IMD, 4401
bytes, mode 0711, dated 7 August 1982.  It has no header, as detab and
compress have none: see ../newuser/README.  The runtime is Whitesmith's, c.ent
at 1149 and c.ents at 0ffc.

Its own code is main at 0132 and the two message routines, the usage message
at 034d and the can't-open message at 0384; the strings are "usage: expand [
input [ output ] ]." at 010e and ": can't open." at 0376.  After that is the
library: finit at 03b8, which attaches a stream to a file
descriptor, then getc at 052c and putc at 05c6, and open and create at 0f80
and 0d57.  create is creat(name, 0777); open is the system call and nothing
more.

main takes up to two names.  With none, the standard input and output are
used, each handed to finit again as it is; with one, it is the input and the
output stays the standard output; with two, the second is created.  More
than two is the usage message, and a name that will not open is the name and
": can't open." - on the standard error, newline-ended - and an exit with
status 0.

The work is a loop over blocks, and the block formats are compress's - see
../compress/README.  A byte n that is not 0377 is a literal count: n bytes
are copied.  The byte 0377 is a run: the next byte is the value and the one
after it the count, and the value is written that many times.  An end of file
anywhere ends the run of the program, quietly, with what has been written so
far written - a truncated literal, a run missing its count.  A literal count of
0 is a block of nothing.  The manual says expand is not guaranteed to do
anything sensible with input that did not come from compress, and it is
no more careful than that.

The one thing to know about the original is that it does not work with one
name.  With one argument the output stream is never attached - only the input
is, by the code at 0186 - and the run never finishes: in usersim it does not
come back.  compress has the same thing, and there the sim reports a stack
overflow at pc 0.  The manual says one name is read and the expansion written
to the standard output; expand.c does what the manual says.

The files are binary and read a byte at a time out of a buffer, as the
original does, and the output is created 0777.  The exit status is 0 for a
clean run and 1 for a mistake; the original's was whatever was in BC at the
end of main for a clean run and 0 for a mistake.

Checked in usersim against the original, byte for byte, on what compress
makes from a file of long runs (98539 bytes to 11529), a file of random bytes,
an empty file, small cases at each boundary, and the binary of compress
itself; and on input that is not compress's - 4000 bytes of the bytes that
matter to it, a literal that ends short, a run that ends short - and on
every error message.
